Z||000014||DJRice||01-30-2001||10:05 AM||||Hmm.  Did your mechanic swap the heads, intake, etc. off the Fiero motor?  If not, you could have some fuel pressure woes.  Your mechanic should look up the specified fuel pressure and check it at the intake.  <P>If you got the engine, trans, and ECM from the beretta, then you should be in pretty good shape.  <P>Its hard to believe that your mechanic would do an engine swap and not give the new motor a standard tune up before dropping it in...<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000015||COUNTIBLIS||01-30-2001||10:46 AM||||No we used everything from the beretta setup except for the transmission.  Yeah I found that hard to believe also about the tune-up.  Would you need to change the fuel pump?  I think that this is the problem.  How difficult is it to change the fuel pressure?  <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/count-sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Black88GT||01-30-2001||11:05 AM||||Well its pretty hard to change the fuel pressure.  Maybe if you got an adj. fuel pressure regulator.<P>I never saw an answer if its aluminum head or not.  <P>Why did the mechanic use all of the 3.1 stuff?  It would have been much easier to use the Fiero stuff, not to mention you keep the stock appearance.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||terryk||01-30-2001||01:20 PM||||Frankly, with the questions your mechanic wants help on, I'm not sure I'd let them work on the car let alone do the swap.<P>It's not the trans unless your description of the symptoms is wrong. It sounds like you have either an ignition problem (coil packs, wires/plugs) or clogged injectors. <P>If you are in park and idling, does it wind up nice with no miss? Does it clear up once you start rolling?<P>As for the FP, unless you are using a 4 cylinder FP, it should be ok. Throw an AC gauge on the fuel rail (an R12 gauge will fit) and measure the pressure. If it's above 35 PSI, that's not the problem. 39-45 PSI is a nice range and the ECM can compensate.<P>The easiest way to troubleshoot this is with a scanner. The INT and BLM will tell you tons about the fuel pressure and delivery.<P>You can always do the paper clip trick. Bend 6 paper clips into a U and insert one under each coil wire. If a wire/plug is bad, the paper clip becomes the shortest path and will arc to another clip (it's mate). That leads to the problem. If the wires/plugs are good, they won't arc (assuming they aren't .100" apart). Pull the plugs and check the insulators for carbon tracking. Check the coil packs for the same. If you find any, replace the plug and the wire, not just one. Very common problem with DIS.||||reg||1
